.TH ARES_QUERY_DNSSEC 3 "28 September 2026"
.SH NAME
ares_query_dnssec \- Initiate a DNS query and validate the answer with DNSSEC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ares.h>

typedef void (*ares_callback_dnsrec)(void *arg, ares_status_t status,
                                     size_t timeouts,
                                     const ares_dns_record_t *dnsrec);

ares_status_t ares_query_dnssec(ares_channel_t      *channel,
                                const char          *name,
                                ares_dns_rec_type_t  type,
                                ares_callback_dnsrec callback,
                                void                *arg);
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The \fBares_query_dnssec(3)\fP function initiates a DNS query of class IN for
the name
.I name
and the type
.I type
on the name service channel identified by
.IR channel ,
and validates the answer with DNSSEC (RFC 4033, RFC 4034, RFC 4035 and
RFC 5155).  Like with \fBares_query_dnsrec(3)\fP, the name is used as given, no
search domains are applied.

The answer is validated from the trust anchors of the DNS root zone (the
KSK-2017 and KSK-2024 keys published by IANA) down to the name.  The DNSKEY and
DS records needed for this are queried from the servers of the channel with the
DO and CD bits set.  The servers are not trusted, they only need to pass on
DNSSEC records.  c-ares has no cryptography of its own: signatures are verified
and hashes calculated with the functions the application sets with
\fBares_set_dnssec_crypto_functions(3)\fP.  Signatures made with the SHA-1
based algorithms RSASHA1 and
RSASHA1-NSEC3-SHA1 are not accepted (RFC 9905), zones signed only with them are
treated as unsigned.  The work a validation does is limited, as protection
against resource exhaustion attacks.

When the validation is complete or has failed, the ares library will invoke
.IR callback ,
the same way as for \fBares_query_dnsrec(3)\fP.  The callback argument
.I timeouts
is the total number of timeouts of all the queries sent.

The record
.I dnsrec
passed to the callback is not the response of a server: it is built from the
validated data only.  It has the question, and when the answer was validated,
the CNAME and DNAME links followed from the question name to the final name (as
CNAME records) and the answer records in the answer section.  It has no
signatures, and no authority or additional section records except as described
below.  Its flags and response code tell the result of the validation:
.TP 12
.B Secure
The answer, or the nonexistence of the name or type, is proven authentic.  The
.B ARES_FLAG_AD
flag is set and the response code is
.B ARES_RCODE_NOERROR
or
.BR ARES_RCODE_NXDOMAIN .
.TP 12
.B Insecure
The answer is proven to come from a part of the DNS that is not signed.  The
.B ARES_FLAG_AD
flag is not set and the response code is
.B ARES_RCODE_NOERROR
or
.BR ARES_RCODE_NXDOMAIN .
.TP 12
.B Bogus
The answer should be signed but the validation failed, like for a missing or
bad signature or a broken chain of trust.  The response code is
.B ARES_RCODE_SERVFAIL
and the additional section has an OPT record with an Extended DNS Error option
(\fBARES_OPT_PARAM_EXTENDED_DNS_ERROR\fP, RFC 8914) with the info code 6
(DNSSEC Bogus) and the reason as text.
.TP 12
.B Indeterminate
The validation could not be completed, like when a query failed.  Like for
bogus, but with the info code 0 (Other Error).
.PP

Applications that need a proven answer, like for DANE (RFC 6698), must check
for the
.B ARES_FLAG_AD
flag.

The callback argument
.I status
is the status for the record as for \fBares_query_dnsrec(3)\fP, like
.B ARES_ENOTFOUND
for NXDOMAIN and
.B ARES_ESERVFAIL
for a bogus or indeterminate answer.  Other errors are passed without a record:
.TP 19
.B ARES_EFORMERR
The name could not be parsed, or the type cannot be validated (like ANY, OPT or
RRSIG).
.TP 19
.B ARES_ENOTIMP
No cryptographic functions are set on the channel.
.TP 19
.B ARES_ENOMEM
Memory was exhausted.
.TP 19
.B ARES_ECANCELLED
The query was cancelled.
.TP 19
.B ARES_EDESTRUCTION
The name service channel
.I channel
is being destroyed; the query will not be completed.
.PP

.SH RETURN VALUES
\fBares_query_dnssec(3)\fP returns
.B ARES_SUCCESS
when the query was started, and otherwise the error that is also passed to the
callback.  When
.I channel
or
.I callback
is NULL,
.B ARES_EFORMERR
is returned and no callback is invoked.

.SH AVAILABILITY
\fBares_query_dnssec(3)\fP was introduced in c-ares 1.35.0.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ares_set_dnssec_crypto_functions (3),
.BR ares_query_dnsrec (3),
.BR ares_process (3),
.BR ares_dns_record (3)
